      <text x="194" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#a32d2d">Invest to unblock</text>
      <text x="194" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#a32d2d">High value, hard to build today</text>
      <text x="426" y="70" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#1a6b32">Build first ★</text>
      <text x="426" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#1a6b32">High value, buildable now</text>
      <text x="194" y="193" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#525252">Park or drop</text>
      <text x="194" y="209" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#525252">Low value, hard</text>
      <text x="426" y="193" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="#633806">Quick win, lower return</text>
      <text x="426" y="209" text-anchor="middle" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="#633806">Low value, easy</text>
      <text x="85" y="270" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#888">Low</text>
      <text x="526" y="270" text-anchor="end" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#888">High</text>
      <text x="90" y="252" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#888">Low</text>
      <text x="90" y="36" font-family="IBM Plex Sans,sans-serif" font-size="10" fill="#888">High</text>
    </svg>
    <div class="visual-caption">Score every candidate on value potential, data readiness, technical feasibility, and strategic fit. The scoring is the deliverable — it's what makes your recommendation defensible when a leader pushes back.</div>
  </div>

  <div class="artifact-section">
    <div class="artifact-section-label">Artifact</div>
    <div class="artifact-grid">
      <div class="artifact-card">
        <div class="a-type">PPT · Medium priority</div>
        <div class="a-title">Prioritization Matrix</div>
        <div class="a-desc">Ranks opportunities by value, feasibility, data readiness, urgency, user impact, and scale to select the MVP.</div>
        <div class="a-links"><a class="a-link" href="https://ibm.box.com/s/39ul2emcnloib0optj35z02ubgoldddj" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">↗ Open example</a><a class="a-link" href="https://ibm.box.com/s/7j0jwsm1lzqvcg47if8gdcza9145kad3" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">↗ Open template</a></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="painpoint">
    <div class="painpoint-label">⚠ Pain point</div>
    <div class="painpoint-title">Picking based on opinions, not evidence</div>
    <div class="painpoint-body">The most common failure here is running a workshop, discussing what "feels" most impactful, and producing a ranking that reflects political momentum more than real opportunity. When that list goes to leadership, it gets overridden — and the team has no data to push back with. The fix requires discipline: use a consistent scoring rubric for every candidate, show the math, and let the numbers drive the conversation. Your recommendation is only as strong as the evidence behind it.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">Also look outside your organization: what are comparable companies doing with similar processes? Benchmarks tell you what's actually achievable — so you're aiming at a real target rather than an improvement to a broken baseline — and they give leadership a concrete frame for why this investment makes sense.</p>

  <div class="painpoint">
    <div class="painpoint-label">⚠ Pain point</div>
    <div class="painpoint-title">Choosing the easy fix instead of the real problem</div>
    <div class="painpoint-body">When time is short, teams gravitate toward workflows that are easy to improve rather than ones that matter. The result is a technically successful build that nobody cares about scaling. Look for root causes when you score candidates — if a workflow's real problem is bad data or a process that shouldn't exist, those issues should show up in the feasibility score, not get papered over.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">Before finalizing the list, check one more thing: confirm that a senior leader will actually commit to each top candidate. Not verbally support it — actually show up to weekly steering meetings, clear blockers, and make hard scope calls when needed. If no one at that level is willing to do that, the project will stall the moment it hits its first obstacle. Drop those candidates now.</p>

  <div class="steps-summary">
    <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">1</div><div class="step-text"><strong>Score all candidates</strong> using the prioritization template — value, feasibility, data readiness, strategic fit. Show the math.</div></div>
    <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">2</div><div class="step-text"><strong>Pull external benchmarks</strong> for each top candidate. What does a leading organization's version of this process look like?</div></div>
    <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">3</div><div class="step-text"><strong>Confirm a sponsor</strong> for the top candidates before finalizing. No sponsor = drop it.</div></div>
    <div class="step-row"><div class="step-num">4</div><div class="step-text"><strong>Sanity-check timelines</strong> against developer capacity. Scope that can't ship in 90 days needs to be flagged now.</div></div>
  </div>


  <div class="case-study">
    <div class="cs-eyebrow"><span class="cs-badge">Case Study</span><span class="cs-context">State University — Scoring four candidate workflows</span></div>
    <p class="cs-prose">The enrollment team ended Engage with four workflows from their process map: document verification, counselor outreach, transcript evaluation, and application processing. Running each through the scoring framework, document verification came out clearly on top — 60% of counselor time spent on it, clean data already in their document system, a confirmed VP of Enrollment sponsor, and a technically simple AI intervention. Outreach ranked second on value but was limited by incomplete CRM data. Transcript evaluation and application processing were parked: low feasibility, no committed sponsor.</p>
    <table class="cs-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Workflow</th><th>Value</th><th>Feasibility</th><th>Data ready</th><th>Sponsor</th><th>Decision</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Doc verification</strong></td><td>●●●●●</td><td>●●●●○</td><td>●●●●○</td><td>✓ VP Enrollment</td><td class="go">Build first ★</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Outreach prioritization</td><td>●●●●○</td><td>●●●○○</td><td>●●○○○</td><td>✓ VP Enrollment</td><td class="wave2">Wave 2</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Transcript evaluation</td><td>●●●○○</td><td>●●○○○</td><td>●○○○○</td><td>—</td><td class="park">Parked</td></tr>
        <tr><td>App processing</td><td>●●○○○</td><td>●●○○○</td><td>●○○○○</td><td>—</td><td class="park">Parked</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="ready">
    <span class="ready-icon">✓</span>
    <div class="ready-body">
      <h4>Ready to move to Discover</h4>
      <p>You have a scored list with one clear winner, a written rationale, a named sponsor, and everything else explicitly parked or dropped. You're moving forward on evidence, not enthusiasm.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>


<!-- ═══════════════════ DISCOVER ═══════════════════ -->
<div class="phase-section">
  <div class="phase-label">
    <span class="phase-tag tag-discover">Discover</span>
    <span class="phase-step-context">Steps 3 &amp; 4 · Workflow Analysis · Solution Design</span>
    <span class="challenge-pip">Prioritization &amp; Roadmap</span>
  </div>

  <h2>Prove the opportunity is real. Then define exactly what you're building.</h2>

  <p class="prose">Discover has two jobs. First, you validate that the workflow you chose is actually worth transforming — not because it scored well on paper, but because the evidence holds up when you look closely. Second, if it does, you define precisely what you're going to build. Both are distinct. Don't skip the first because you're confident; don't rush the second because you're excited.</p>

  <p class="prose">Your executive sponsor's commitment from Engage now has a concrete meaning: they are committing to provide access to the process owners and SMEs you need across both steps. Without that access — real conversations with the people who do the work, not documentation reviews — neither the Workflow Analysis nor the requirements will be grounded in reality.</p>

  <!-- SUB-PHASE 1: WORKFLOW ANALYSIS -->
  <div class="sub-phase">
    <div class="sub-phase-label">
      <span class="sub-phase-number">Step 3</span>
      <h3>Workflow Analysis — Validate the opportunity</h3>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">The Workflow Analysis is a time-boxed investigation — typically two weeks — into the workflow you selected. You need process SMEs who know the current state in detail (not just their manager's version of it), and a tech SME who can assess data access and integration paths as you go. You're gathering: how much the workflow costs to run today, what skills it requires, what the key performance indicators are, what best-in-class looks like at peer organizations, and — most importantly — what's actually causing it to perform poorly. Your output is a recommendation: proceed to Solution Design, or return to the prioritized list.</p>

  <div class="painpoint">
    <div class="painpoint-label">⚠ Pain point</div>
    <div class="painpoint-title">The go/no-go decision that never gets made</div>
    <div class="painpoint-body">The hardest moment in this phase isn't the analysis — it's having an honest conversation about whether to proceed. Teams naturally want to move forward. Sponsors have staked credibility on their workflow selection. The mechanism to protect against this is simple: book the go/no-go meeting with the steering committee <em>before the analysis starts</em>, and brief the sponsor that they may hear a recommendation to stop. A no-go isn't a failure — it means the investigation worked. Without a hard date and explicit setup, the meeting drifts, the decision softens, and a marginal workflow goes into design and becomes a failed build months later.</div>
  </div>

