  <!-- SUB-PHASE 2: SOLUTION DESIGN -->
  <div class="sub-phase">
    <div class="sub-phase-label">
      <span class="sub-phase-number">Step 4</span>
      <h3>Solution Design — Define the vision, scope the build</h3>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">Solution Design brings in the product owner alongside the process and tech SMEs already in the room. Together you answer three questions in sequence, and the sequence matters: you can't scope the MVP until you have the end-state vision, and you can't write requirements until you have the MVP scope.</p>

  <p class="prose"><strong>First: define the end-state vision.</strong> Describe the fully transformed workflow — what would it look like if you redesigned it from scratch with today's AI capabilities, unconstrained by how things work today? This isn't a product spec; it's a shared picture of where you're ultimately heading. The end-state vision is what prevents the MVP from becoming the final destination. Every scope decision you make after this should be tested against it: does this move us toward that vision, or away from it?</p>

  <p class="prose"><strong>Second: define the 90-day scope.</strong> The MVP is the smallest version of the solution that meaningfully tests whether the core hypothesis is right. It shouldn't try to deliver the full end-state vision — that would take longer and risk proving nothing. The goal is to build something real users can interact with, generate evidence against your baseline metrics, and support a data-driven go/no-go decision at Day 90. A well-scoped MVP also builds a reusable foundation: integrations, data pipelines, and architecture patterns that the next workflow can build on rather than rebuild from scratch. Design for modularity now.</p>

  <p class="prose"><strong>Third: write the out-of-scope list.</strong> This is as important as the in-scope list. Everything that isn't explicitly parked becomes an implicit yes, which is how delivery timelines slip. The out-of-scope list is also the seed of your post-MVP backlog — it should include target dates and owners so it doesn't disappear after Day 90.</p>

  <div class="painpoint">
    <div class="painpoint-label">⚠ Pain point</div>
    <div class="painpoint-title">Handing over requirements no one can build from</div>
    <div class="painpoint-body">The most common handoff failure in this entire process happens here. Business teams write requirements that make sense internally but are too conceptual for a development team to act on. The test is simple: give a developer the requirements document and ask if they can write a user story from it without asking you a single clarifying question. If they can't, it's not ready. This isn't about length — it's about specificity: who does what, in what system, with what data, and what does "done" look like.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">Get the scope — both lists — signed off by the executive sponsor before the build team takes over. That sign-off is what gives the project lead standing to hold the line when someone asks to add scope mid-sprint.</p>

