  <h2>Build with a 90-day clock. Decide on data. Set up the next bet.</h2>

  <!-- SUB-PHASE: EXPERIMENTATION -->
  <div class="sub-phase">
    <div class="sub-phase-label">
      <span class="sub-phase-number">Step 5</span>
      <h3>Experimentation — 90-day build and go/no-go</h3>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">Before any code is written, the build team needs to confirm they have everything required to execute. That means reviewing not just the requirements document, but the full project picture: the end-state vision (so they understand what they're building toward), the MVP scope and what's explicitly out, who the SMEs are and how to reach them mid-sprint, the operating model (who makes decisions and at what cadence), and how the engagement model works when a requirement needs to change. If any of those are unclear, surface it in the first two days — not week six.</p>

  <p class="prose">Run the build in two-week sprint cycles. What you test first matters: put the riskiest assumption — the thing most likely to invalidate the whole approach — in Sprint 1, while there's still room to pivot. The steering committee's job is to clear blockers and surface problems, not to receive good news. One transformation lead put it well: <strong>"Red on a chart is good."</strong> A build showing only green is either not measuring the right things or not reporting honestly.</p>

  <p class="prose">Some requirements will be defined or refined mid-sprint. That's expected — users react to working software differently than they react to wireframes. The key is having a clear pipeline from that feedback to the roadmap: new or changed requirements get logged, triaged with the product owner, and either pulled into the current sprint, queued for the next one, or added to the post-MVP backlog. Without that pipeline, the build drifts or the team makes scope decisions unilaterally.</p>

  <div class="painpoint">
    <div class="painpoint-label">⚠ Pain point</div>
    <div class="painpoint-title">Go/no-go metrics set after the build</div>
    <div class="painpoint-body">If you haven't locked the go/no-go criteria before Day 1, they'll be defined after the build — which means they'll be defined to pass. The Day 90 decision needs to answer a pre-agreed question: did this build demonstrate enough value, at enough reliability, to justify scaling? Write that question down. Agree on the data source. Get sponsor sign-off before development starts. A metric with no pre-agreed threshold is decoration, not a decision rule.</div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">Scope reductions during the build are not failures — they're the mechanism that makes 90-day delivery work. What determines whether a de-scope preserves or destroys executive confidence is how it's communicated: show what was cut, why, when it comes back, and what the team is protecting by cutting it.</p>

  <!-- SUB-PHASE: SCALE & ADOPT -->
  <div class="sub-phase" style="margin-top:2rem;">
    <div class="sub-phase-label">
      <span class="sub-phase-number">Step 6</span>
      <h3>Scale &amp; Adopt — After the decision</h3>
    </div>
  </div>

  <p class="prose">The go/no-go at Day 90 is not the end of the roadmap — it's the beginning of the next phase. If it's a go, the post-MVP backlog activates, change management moves into full execution, and the team shifts from building to embedding. If it's a no-go, the documentation of what was learned is the input to the next workflow analysis — and that next cycle will be faster because the reusable infrastructure from this build carries forward.</p>

  <p class="prose">Before the transformation team steps back, two ownership roles need to be named explicitly: a <strong>business owner</strong> who is accountable for adoption, user support, and the ongoing business outcomes of the solution, and a <strong>technical lead</strong> who maintains the infrastructure, manages updates, and handles system-level issues. Ongoing user support and change management is a joint responsibility between these two — neither owns it alone. When these roles aren't named before handoff, the solution degrades quietly.</p>

  <p class="prose">Naming owners is necessary but not sufficient. You also need a definition of "done" that everyone agrees on before the transformation team exits. What does full adoption look like — MECE definition of the user population, target utilization rate, target KPI achievement? What does the full-featured product look like relative to the end-state vision defined in Step 4? These definitions shape the post-90-day roadmap and give the business owner something to manage toward.</p>

